Single-argument FLOOR / ROUND / CEILING / TRUNCATE of a RATIO signal a TYPE ERROR.

STATUS: open.  Discovered while driving the ASDF DEFSYSTEM/OPERATE work
(uiop-bocl-porting.issue): a diagnostic harness computed
(round (/ elapsed internal-time-units-per-second)) — i.e. (round <ratio>) — and
got "TYPE ERROR: expected INTEGER, got RATIO".  Non-blocking for ASDF (ASDF
itself does not depend on it; only the test harness did).

== Symptom ==
Ratio arithmetic (+ - * /) works and ratios print correctly, and the TWO-argument
divide-and-round forms work, but the ONE-argument rounding functions reject a
ratio:

  (/ 7 3)        => 7/3            ; ok
  (+ 1/2 1/3)    => 5/6            ; ok
  (* 1000 1/3)   => 1000/3         ; ok
  (- 5/2 1)      => 3/2            ; ok
  (round 7 3)    => 2              ; ok (two-arg)
  (round 1/3)    => TYPE ERROR: expected INTEGER, got RATIO   ; BUG (want 0, 1/3)
  (floor 1/3)    => TYPE ERROR: expected INTEGER, got RATIO   ; BUG (want 0, 1/3)

CEILING and TRUNCATE almost certainly share the defect (same single-arg path).

== Expected (CLHS 12.2 FLOOR/CEILING/TRUNCATE/ROUND) ==
With a single REAL argument these return (values <integer-quotient> <remainder>):
  (floor 1/3)   => 0, 1/3
  (floor 7/3)   => 2, 1/3
  (floor -1/3)  => -1, 2/3
  (round 1/3)   => 0, 1/3
  (round 5/2)   => 2, 1/2   ; round-half-to-even
  (truncate 7/3)=> 2, 1/3
  (ceiling 1/3) => 1, -2/3

== Likely cause ==
The single-argument FLOOR/ROUND/etc. path assumes an INTEGER (or FLOAT) operand
and does not handle the RATIO case.  For a ratio N/D it can compute the integer
quotient and remainder directly from (FLOOR N D) etc. on the numerator/denominator
(D > 0 by ratio normalisation), then form the fractional remainder as
(- ratio quotient).

== Fix sketch ==
In the prelude (or kernel) FLOOR/CEILING/TRUNCATE/ROUND single-arg handlers, add
a RATIO branch:
  (floor r)   = let q = (floor (numerator r) (denominator r))   -> (values q (- r q))
  (truncate r)= toward zero;  (ceiling r) = (- (floor (- r)));  (round r) = nearest,
  ties to even.
The two-argument (round a b) path already works, so single-arg can delegate:
e.g. (floor r) == (floor (numerator r) (denominator r)) returning the same two
values (quotient + a REMAINDER scaled back to a ratio: (/ rem (denominator r))).

== Repro ==
  bocl --no-init --batch -x '(print (round 1/3))'
  => TYPE ERROR: expected INTEGER, got RATIO

== RESOLVED (v1.5.9) ==
The kernel divop macro (arithmetic.c) has integer/bignum/float branches only; a
ratio fell to the integer path and INTEGER-VALUE'd it -> TYPE ERROR.  Fixed with
prelude wrappers (after the type predicates): FLOOR / CEILING / TRUNCATE / ROUND
capture the kernel divops (*%kernel-floor* ...) and, when NUMBER or DIVISOR is a
ratio, compute exactly via %RATIONAL-DIVOP — q = <kernel two-arg integer divop>
(numerator, denominator) of (/ NUMBER DIVISOR); remainder = (- NUMBER (* q
DIVISOR)).  Integer/float operands take the unchanged kernel path.  Verified all
the issue's expected values, incl. round ties-to-even ((round 5/2)=>2,1/2) and the
two-arg ratio form ((floor 1/2 1/3)=>1,1/6).
Also fixed the same defect in MOD / REM (non-blocking, same class): a ratio
operand yields the FLOOR / TRUNCATE remainder respectively.
New test_numeric_extras "ratio FLOOR/ROUND/...".  Full unit suite (42) green.
